From bf29546d955aa6b7a02d39b09d72fc526b13650a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nyfe <hornsons21@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 13:32:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Ironsight is Sightline
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The name fits what it turned into. What it does now is stand at the point where
an agent asks whether it may do something, and answer — which is a line of sight
onto the work, not a weapon sight on a target.

The sweep is mechanical: crates, binaries, the data directory, the project
directory, the environment variable, the docs. Two things were not mechanical.

Nothing breaks for a project that has not been renamed. `.ironsight/` is still
read wherever `.sightline/` would be — a project's constitution and checks are
committed with its code, and there is no upgrade step anyone would think to run.
The current name wins when both exist, because a project mid-rename means the
one it is moving to. `IRONSIGHT_DATA_DIR` is still honoured, so a script or a
unit file written before today does not silently point somewhere else and lose
the fleet, and the data directory migrates itself in place the way it already
did from nyfe-scope.

And the architecture document already used "Sightline" for the thing you type
into, which the product has now taken. That is simply the prompt: you type into
Sightline.

The GitHub remote still says ironsight. Renaming it is outward-facing and not
mine to do.
